comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-14T20:17:55.068Z","harness:claude-code","Narrowed 2026-11-14 by PR #1732: list flags no longer lose Commander-parsed values when an unrelated flag reaches the loose parser (readOrderedLooseListValues now falls back when no occurrence names the flag). What remains here is only the same-flag mix of attached and bare occurrences of a repeatable switch."
  "2026-11-23T08:47:02.325Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation evidence (2026-11-23). orderArgvRepeatableSwitchOccurrences (src/sdk/extension/command-options.ts) re-derives the occurrences of every declared repeatable value-less boolean switch from the captured COMMAND_OPTION_TOKENS with the same scanArgvScope walk that refuseConflictingArgvOptionSpellings uses. resolveArgvSpelling gained a tracks selector (scalar flags for the conflict check, repeatable switches for order), and the occurrence sink now receives whether a value was attached with '=', so an attached --many=bogus is still judged as an explicit value by refuseMalformedAttachedSwitchValues while a bare occurrence stays presence. extractCommandScopedOptions (src/cli/runtime/invocation-options.ts) passes the reordered source to the shared coercer; collectCommandOptionScopes is now shared by both callers.\n\nBuilt-CLI cases added to the gh1726 spec: --many=false --many is [false, true], --many --many=false is [true, false], --many --many=false --many is [true, false, true], --many=true --many=false is [true, false], and --many --from-tracker --many=false is [true] because --from-tracker consumes the token after it. Negative control: with the reordering disabled the first mixed case fails with 'expected [ false ] to deeply equal [ false, true ]'. Unit spec covers short alias, ancestor scope, --no-many, the unchanged-source paths and the unordered coercer result beside each ordered one.\n"
  "2026-11-23T10:16:01.262Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 1 on PR #1772 (Codex, 2026-11-23). Fixed: (1) the replacement dropped every loose occurrence under the switch's alias keys, so with -S aliasing --many beside an independent strict --s, --many --s=invalid lost the s occurrence and its strict refusal; replacement now uses the coercer's own key ownership (a key that is another declaration's canonical key stays with it). (2) The loose parser folds --Many=false onto many while the scan looked spellings up verbatim, so the folded value was dropped and --many --Many=invalid escaped strict validation; the scan now folds an unregistered leaf spelling with the loose parser's key normalization. command-options.spec.ts covers both. Deferred: the same switch registered on a command and its subcommand is merged in scope order, not argv order, when the parent consumes a bare occurrence; filed as pm-302reu with the alignment approach.\n"
  "2026-11-23T10:55:36.637Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2 on PR #1772 (Codex, Greptile, CodeRabbit, 2026-11-23). (1) Codex: the scan re-derived loose values from raw argv adjacency, so --many --Many --source x false read --Many as bare true while the loose parser, reading the tokens Commander leaves (--Many false), recorded false. The scan now only reports, in argv order, Commander's own occurrences and the slots of loose occurrences; every loose occurrence keeps the loose parser's value and attached mark. When the scan and the loose parser disagree on the number of loose occurrences, every value is kept, loose ones first. (2) Greptile and CodeRabbit: the folded-spelling fallback read a registered short group -mm as the long --mm before Commander's short-group split; folding now applies to long -- spellings only. Negative control: without the guard the -mm --many=false case delivers [false] instead of [true, true, false]. command-options.spec.ts covers both.\n"
